#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161713 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/id #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12462" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.28$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.42$ | $0.18$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.28.$ Likewise, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.4 \\cdot 0.7 = 0.28. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.12=0.4\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.42=0.6\\cdot 0.7={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.18=0.6\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161714 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/id #uuid "4e12d6b6-5771-5e54-8f16-7899f82d90bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12463" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,2).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_Y(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.55$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161715 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/id #uuid "1875d098-95f6-50a7-b069-56865207b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12464" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.24$ | $0.06$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.56$ | $0.14$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_X(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f_Y(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = P(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x) \\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y (y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P({X = 0}, {Y = 0}) = f(0,0) = 0.24.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0) \\cdot P(Y=0) = f_X(0) \\cdot f_Y(0) = 0.3 \\cdot 0.8 = 0.24. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =0.56=0.7\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(0,1) & =0.06=0.3\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.14=0.7\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P({X = x}, {Y = y}) = P(X=x) \\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea91f7c-b404-57ce-b26b-09691c505bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570146} {:answer/id #uuid "4af19015-99ba-5b62-a940-6de9e9bbcfba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570255} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7d5284-3afb-5213-bddf-0f8f5b688a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570285} {:answer/id #uuid "65a140a9-45f0-5e30-b05a-59065a2072cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570302} {:answer/id #uuid "6600c5ed-cae9-564e-9fb1-8fa6c43b7f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65a140a9-45f0-5e30-b05a-59065a2072cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570302} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0c463fc-1015-5085-92f8-1bd4d3071032" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dd8077d-1403-5bd2-b29d-34936c801dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-143329" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.11.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.55\\cdot 0.2=0.11.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =0.44=0.55\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.09=0.45\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.36=0.45\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99c0be41-4842-5d84-9243-b20970ca65f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464270} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3a1711-1ce6-584c-8666-fd81316fdd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3a1711-1ce6-584c-8666-fd81316fdd9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464271} :answer-field/id #uuid "19e8b9f1-2710-5c4d-97d6-670c3ce72f83" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6dc7de0-fb70-572e-9395-bf45e4359ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464272} {:answer/id #uuid "3e54788f-563d-5897-822c-4ffcb07e00ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e54788f-563d-5897-822c-4ffcb07e00ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464273} :answer-field/id #uuid "a82999e5-03fb-56c4-8ba2-aa587b618145" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465070} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a65e48-8a48-5658-be41-145c5aab2fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464274} {:answer/id #uuid "3d42055b-43bb-5621-bee4-f237edcf65e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d42055b-43bb-5621-bee4-f237edcf65e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464275} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d508279-cf49-5ea0-a9cb-97c308df61f8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a347e7c2-3db8-5058-926f-eeec20d91744" :question/math-academy-id "q-143331" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P(X=2,Y=1)=P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n- **II.** $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- **III.** $X$ and $Y$ are independent\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.6$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=2,Y=1)=f(2,1)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.2\\cdot 0.75=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.6=0.8\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.2=0.8\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(2,2) & =0.05=0.2\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd7e9f64-cbbd-5bbd-8464-3f61b6f11586" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464288} {:answer/id #uuid "b46bc96b-ac7e-5f3f-a9a5-4cdfc8aba11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464289}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b46bc96b-ac7e-5f3f-a9a5-4cdfc8aba11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464289} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca347041-dadc-56e2-bbf1-f6ebc4c70e09" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e699ac2-764b-512c-8f0d-5172d00686da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464290} {:answer/id #uuid "006bf10f-88fe-5637-949f-4aa29407bc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e699ac2-764b-512c-8f0d-5172d00686da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464290} :answer-field/id #uuid "83f7c971-351f-53ff-be7e-c7304f5c86bf" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17db8ae0-64d6-5514-9124-5fa3d5e8dba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464292} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a98f0c-64a9-57fe-b332-0fefcb246690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464293}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17db8ae0-64d6-5514-9124-5fa3d5e8dba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464292} :answer-field/id #uuid "da33b576-bcf0-5c09-83dd-58d6caaeb72b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "743eb09d-bf29-57ce-b05b-46da6dccd3f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-143338" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $P(X=3,Y=2)=P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n- **II.** $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n- **III.** $X$ and $Y$ are independent\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | $Y=6$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.16$ | $0.01$ | $0.03$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.64$ | $0.09$ | $0.07$ | $0.8$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.64.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.8\\cdot 0.8=0.64.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =0.16=0.2\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.01\\ne 0.2\\cdot 0.1={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15cf2687-956c-5a04-a0e5-2aa85321d477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570165} {:answer/id #uuid "2092959b-1b46-5391-a673-2f6b71e1a7f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570182} {:answer/id #uuid "5796432d-3e16-5dac-aa46-18f20d2fd823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570279} {:answer/id #uuid "b09ee838-ec83-5170-b0f1-28570d5a0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570445} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbe36f5-f029-58af-b6c8-d0c6956746a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b09ee838-ec83-5170-b0f1-28570d5a0b7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570445} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9a7c0fb-9ef9-5eb1-9b4f-38bbe8edbdcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86447238-489f-5596-b009-12655e2594dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-143436" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=3,Y=2)=P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nComputing the marginal probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y,$ we obtain the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.8$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.8$ | $0.2$ | $1$ |\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=3,Y=2)=f(3,2)=0.1.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(3)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.2\\cdot 0.8=0.16.\\,\\times\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=3,Y=2)\\ne P(X=3)\\cdot P(Y=2),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of those listed.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab2a799f-1de3-5949-9d28-e92b9b2c1853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159531} {:answer/id #uuid "bf333e3f-1e9a-512c-9ee9-c815a6ddb929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159532} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b5d122-7f9e-5765-8d55-ba39fdfa2b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159533} {:answer/id #uuid "4536fe93-9e65-5770-ab9c-9ff6f05a6008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159534} {:answer/id #uuid "52fc1410-4377-5834-a089-600330682922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159535}] :answer-field/id #uuid "20cfccdf-0f62-55e2-92b1-68319ea00399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159530}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e60a4ff-2b15-51cb-bace-fa72d363609f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143438" :question/problem "The joint probability distribution $f(x, y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y = 0$ | $Y = 1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.25$ | $0.55$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n1. $P(X = 0, Y = 1) = P(X = 0) \\cdot P(Y = 1)$\n2. $P(X = x, Y = y) = P(X = x) \\cdot P(Y = y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n3. $X$ and $Y$ are independent\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3228beb4-ad50-531c-b488-d73c9985ee19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570211} {:answer/id #uuid "5392d1d8-0ead-5406-b4da-fc7f65455500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570271} {:answer/id #uuid "71fff1b0-8e0b-5d4f-b9b9-0f9b67b6b928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570328} {:answer/id #uuid "9009a014-79e2-5f7f-b4a9-236a79c15e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.08" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570376} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b587ec-54f7-53f1-bb48-172fcb853d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5392d1d8-0ead-5406-b4da-fc7f65455500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570271} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c35ad51-9dbb-5b36-83e3-01a4846b5800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1200d290-e6bf-5cb3-acb4-bada082b8f96" :question/math-academy-id "q-149649" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(2,5).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th></tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,5) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(5) \\\\  & =0.2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e2ab1f-ce9b-5969-b153-8c952635697f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491606} {:answer/id #uuid "6c01961e-283c-5e82-89cd-af921b8b18cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491647} {:answer/id #uuid "79b00643-d756-5605-96ea-7aef73ff16fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.06" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491665} {:answer/id #uuid "bfceb24c-fc28-5771-a384-73a046579e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491785} {:answer/id #uuid "f4eeb262-2789-575f-8f08-0e1aa6046842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.09" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c01961e-283c-5e82-89cd-af921b8b18cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491647} :answer-field/id #uuid "5826e975-df63-51e0-a67a-1164fd2d1f8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c1df99e-2ead-55b4-87fb-2ae2dcf226a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149654" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(3,0).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e0926c-dc16-5c6f-aef8-a5999f210009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.455" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570120} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbc3c76-06e3-5bcf-a5f8-8ef75b6a0b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570150} {:answer/id #uuid "2137f84b-7e04-5e1f-b4e8-004de4b809f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.245" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570184} {:answer/id #uuid "693f3501-9ea8-52a8-b120-f969af9507c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570311} {:answer/id #uuid "84cfd073-9456-54ff-bfb1-9ff4d427e723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.195" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbc3c76-06e3-5bcf-a5f8-8ef75b6a0b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Impossible to determine using the given information." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570150} :answer-field/id #uuid "47dc876f-fe20-575f-9f83-7dfac231f314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1222ccd8-a96c-520b-9c55-3b7bc8a21989" :question/math-academy-id "q-149655" :question/problem "The marginal mass functions of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(6,4).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Since we don't know whether $X$ and $Y$ are independent, it is impossible to determine the joint distribution of $X$ and $Y$ using only the given information."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9d0c57-960a-50ad-8fa7-9d10ca6901e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570143} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccbb2ec-9296-5be5-baf9-880ebc4a387f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570201} {:answer/id #uuid "84d27243-3576-50aa-aba0-4aa1d2550b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570357} {:answer/id #uuid "a73d0423-625d-5c20-8c7f-7d3f2dd4cd92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570421} {:answer/id #uuid "c92f5681-4932-52f0-ad86-222d7ef82f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b9d0c57-960a-50ad-8fa7-9d10ca6901e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570143} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbd75960-a5dc-507f-9c7b-443c5b7e70cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3efec416-c341-5cd9-8d7e-9c91c7f8fc1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-162826" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.38$ | $0.32$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.02$ | $0.28$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.32.$ However, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.7\\cdot 0.6=0.42.\\,\\times\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nP(X=0,Y=1)\\ne P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1),\n$$\n\nand $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0777157f-29ea-5e24-9e8e-6b9a24e51747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481580} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1ed2e1-55cd-57e9-aa50-68794e5c6eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481584} {:answer/id #uuid "162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481588} {:answer/id #uuid "da46d0be-42c1-57a1-ab4a-e8a9512a5cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481634} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2dac8c-ea1a-540c-93ab-6f8d5b85be8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "162d01d4-4c05-520f-a7ba-901b79767c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481588} :answer-field/id #uuid "287256f7-b082-5a2a-854b-414b7b8a37a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481653}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34f7a331-3a09-5214-b793-a1233bb14dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162827" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=-2$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.16$ | $0.09$ | $0.05$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.44$ | $0.21$ | $0.05$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=0)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=0)=f(0,0)=0.09.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.3\\cdot 0.3=0.09.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-2) & =0.16\\ne 0.3\\cdot 0.6={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(-2) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7b724bb-12b8-5cef-8faa-67e3abc1c662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383584} {:answer/id #uuid "51d99381-4595-5f23-81c1-ffc85c5a030f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383585} {:answer/id #uuid "4800c0fd-9e5e-5a65-b380-162e5926682f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383586} {:answer/id #uuid "44d94f33-c441-5b51-b090-7e6f8e9f1105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383587} {:answer/id #uuid "d08f7d94-ffb7-5a2a-a611-ed01f3840d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d08f7d94-ffb7-5a2a-a611-ed01f3840d77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383588} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb5fdd6-9f39-54e0-b178-a92abdd7891e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5e76acd4-e746-50cd-aa7b-13042adeab92" :question/math-academy-id "q-162900" :question/problem "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=5$ | $0.12$ | $∗$ | $?$ |\n| $X=7$ | $0.24$ | $∗$ | $∗$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.36$ | $0.64$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(X=5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(5,0) & ={f}_{X}(5)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) \\\\ P(X=5,Y=0) & =P(X=5)\\cdot P(Y=0) \\\\ 0.12 & =P(X=5)\\cdot 0.36 \\\\ P(X=5) & =\\frac{0.12}{0.36} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570721 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19aa25ad-336b-5766-a733-09397663c89a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570174} {:answer/id #uuid "2545460e-d87d-59a9-896f-f4804c477441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570191} {:answer/id #uuid "7f50fbea-844a-5239-b2f9-f71e2cc1ef2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570348} {:answer/id #uuid "a099181c-9c17-5544-8a65-42b3b9368d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570406} {:answer/id #uuid "efebf525-cba0-577d-92c8-aacdbfe1f3b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a099181c-9c17-5544-8a65-42b3b9368d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570406} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbc1988e-2e0c-53fc-9f2e-80ceecb51b3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38788558-45ed-5ac8-b24c-5c511e7f90ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-162902" :question/problem "The partially complete joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is below. \n\n| $f$ | $Y=5$ | $Y=7$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $∗$ | $∗$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $?$ | $∗$ | $1$ |\n\nWhat is the value of $P(Y=5)?$" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our random variables are independent, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,5) & ={f}_{X}(2)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(5) \\\\ P(X=2,Y=5) & =P(X=2)\\cdot P(Y=5) \\\\ 0.09 & =0.45\\cdot P(Y=5) \\\\ P(Y=5) & =\\frac{0.09}{0.45} \\\\  & =0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e932aa-df02-5ddf-9c65-deb82d4c7df1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570124} {:answer/id #uuid "025fce0b-097b-5731-a74e-5ae7056d6398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570125} {:answer/id #uuid "32991881-c8fb-5dc7-bdb3-41ca8ebbdef3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570213} {:answer/id #uuid "437956a9-2047-5bcd-80f5-8a0239806bdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570240} {:answer/id #uuid "c5470c69-c4ff-596e-ab51-bfd9340f48f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "025fce0b-097b-5731-a74e-5ae7056d6398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570125} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6c7aa94-526c-5068-b2c9-6d8c4ba852ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e322b76b-ff51-56ef-b559-1a8882734867" :question/math-academy-id "q-163167" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.15$ | $0.05$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=1)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=1)=f(0,1)=0.3.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=1)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1)=0.6\\cdot 0.5=0.3.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Checking some possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2) & =0.15\\ne 0.6\\cdot 0.3={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)\\ne P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ are *not* independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570775 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0445cf07-339e-533c-8d42-e149c6fc2cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570129} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc56465-1d50-5315-918d-4e291db03326" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570144} {:answer/id #uuid "55420914-c12f-5597-a7c1-3b23dc38ce54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570274} {:answer/id #uuid "621af6a9-adbe-50db-806c-fe5e8f2b32bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570296} {:answer/id #uuid "68910a4f-8fa8-58d5-afc0-03e3a87b1e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0445cf07-339e-533c-8d42-e149c6fc2cbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570129} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c90bc9-5310-58df-8a7a-9bd5771aabfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d05ba4df-d0b9-5ab9-9679-a4617e87b3ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-163603" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=2)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=2)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=2)=f(0,2)=0.15.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=2)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2)=0.6\\cdot 0.25=0.15.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,4) & =0.45=0.6\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,2) & =0.1=0.4\\cdot 0.25={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(2) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,4) & =0.3=0.4\\cdot 0.75={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(4) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756e4d55-1378-53e7-a647-4bb957d8354e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570336} {:answer/id #uuid "8786f7bb-119b-5478-811b-8f6c4d6bf3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570363} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fad4c1-adb5-5533-b833-7a6d9b1b1743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570411} {:answer/id #uuid "c70e303f-f758-5b87-8345-25eead5e7229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570472} {:answer/id #uuid "cfe90f05-b84c-55e2-b56e-b9d05ab09410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "756e4d55-1378-53e7-a647-4bb957d8354e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570336} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d65433b-d81b-571d-acc8-1bea6c4268fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ad1bec0-7cb2-5524-90ce-c8dd7afd9873" :question/math-academy-id "q-163615" :question/problem "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.07$ | $0.28$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.13$ | $0.52$ |\n\nThe joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given above, while the marginal mass functions for these variables are shown below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P(X=0,Y=0)=P(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)$\n\nII. $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent" :question/worked-solution "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the table, we obtain that $P(X=0,Y=0)=f(0,0)=0.07.$ On the other hand, \n\n$$\nP(X=0)\\cdot P(Y=0)={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0)=0.35\\cdot 0.2=0.07.\\,✓\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Checking all other possible products, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =0.28=0.35\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(0)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,0) & =0.13=0.65\\cdot 0.2={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(0) & ✓ \\\\ f(1,1) & =0.52=0.65\\cdot 0.8={f}_{X}(1)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, our random variables $X$ and $Y$ must be independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
